## 4. Use the custom URL in your style JSON

Your style can stay exactly the same as on the web:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my-vault-tiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": [
        "vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"
      ],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 14
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "my-layer",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "my-vault-tiles",
      "source-layer": "my_layer_name",
      "paint": {
        "fill-color": "#ff6600",
        "fill-opacity": 0.6
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Load the style exactly as you would any other JSON:

```kotlin
mapboxMap?.setStyle(Style.Builder().fromUri("asset://my_style.json"))
```

When the style engine reaches the `tiles` URL, the native core will invoke your `VaultTileRequestHandler`, you decrypt the tile, and the map renders it – all without ever hitting the network.

---

## 5. Gotchas & Best Practices

| Issue | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|--------------------|
| **Handler not called** | The handler must be registered **before** the first `MapView` is created. If you call `MapLibre.getInstance` *after* inflating a `MapView`, the view will have been built with the default options and your handler will be ignored. |
| **Returning `null` response** | In MapLibre’s `HttpRequestHandler` you must **never** return `null`. Instead return a `HttpResponse` with a status code of `0` (or any non-2xx) and a `null` body – this tells the core to fall back to its built-in network stack. |
| **Large tiles / slow decryption** | Decryption runs on a thread pool shared by the native core. If you block that pool for too long you will see “tiles stuck loading”. Cache the decrypted bytes (e.g. LRU memory cache or a small on-disk cache) and keep the crypto operation lightweight. |
| **Thread-safety** | The handler may be called concurrently for many tiles. Make any shared objects (e.g. a `Cipher` instance) thread-safe or create a new instance per request. |
| **Content-type** | The native core does not check the MIME type for vector tiles, but if you ever serve raster tiles you can set the `Content-Type` header in the response via `HttpResponse.setHeader("Content-Type", "image/png")`. |
| **Testing** | Use `adb logcat` to print a line inside `handleRequest` – you’ll see it fire for every tile request. You can also enable the SDK’s debug logging: `MapLibre.getInstance(context).setDebugLoggingEnabled(true)`. |
| **Offline only** | If you never need to hit the network, you can also disable the default network stack entirely by returning a `404` for any non-`vault` URL. This prevents accidental leaks. |

---

## 6. Full Minimal Project Skeleton

```
app/
 └─ src/
     └─ main/
         ├─ java/com/example/myapp/
         │   ├─ MyApp.kt                // registers the handler
         │   ├─ VaultTileRequestHandler.kt
         │   ├─ TileVault.kt
         │   └─ CryptoHelper.kt         // your own AES/ChaCha implementation
         └─ res/
             └─ raw/
                 └─ my_style.json       // style that references vault://…
```

**MyApp.kt**

```kotlin
class MyApp : Application() {
    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()
        val vault = TileVault(
            encryptedBlob = File(filesDir, "tiles_blob.dat"),
            crypto = CryptoHelper(SecretKeySpec(myKeyBytes, "AES"))
        )
        val options = MapLibreOptions()
            .httpRequestHandler(VaultTileRequestHandler(vault))
        MapLibre.getInstance(this, options)
    }
}
```

**MainActivity.kt (just to show usage)**

